diagnosis_text:
The user's report of PC0 (20.1.1.x) being unable to reach PC12 (120.1.1.x) is not confirmed by any live evidence. A topology-wide ICMP reachability sweep in Phase 1 shows 0% packet loss in both directions between the two hosts (RTT ≈ 1 ms). The network uses a split routing architecture: an OSPF domain covers r1–r10, and an EIGRP domain (AS 100) covers r12 and r13, with r11 acting as the redistribution boundary. r11 correctly runs both protocols — EIGRP with r12 (112.1.1.2, Full, 11+ min uptime) and r13 (103.1.1.2), and OSPF area 3 with r1 — and redistributes EIGRP routes (including 120.0.0.0/8) into OSPF and connected routes outbound. r1 carries a confirmed OSPF route to 120.0.0.0/8 via r11, and r12 holds a kernel default route covering the return path to 20.x. Phase A (infra_sweep, l2_snapshot) found no ACL rules, no MAC conflicts, and no addressing errors. Phase B tc_snapshot confirmed no traffic shaping. Phase D pressure_sweep found no stress tools or resource spikes. An ospf_snapshot flag on r8 (orphaned area 0.0.0.2) was investigated and found to be a correctly functioning leaf-stub area — r8 holds Full adjacencies with r5 (area 1) and r4 (via VLINK0, area 0), and its area-2 routes appear correctly in r1's routing table. No flap scripts, no carrier history, and no daemon logs indicate any intermittent mechanism. The user's complaint is unconfirmed; the current state is fully healthy.
